Yes. A rotation preserves the volume form on $S^{n-1}$. Here is the proof.

**Setup.** Let $A:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$ be a rotation, i.e. $A\in SO(n)$: a linear map with $A^T A = I$ and $\det A = 1$. Since $A$ is orthogonal, $\|Ax\|=\|x\|$, so $A$ restricts to a diffeomorphism $f:=A|_{S^{n-1}}:S^{n-1}\to S^{n-1}$, with $df_p = A$ at every $p$ (linearity).

**The volume form on $S^{n-1}$.** Let $\omega = dx^1\wedge\cdots\wedge dx^n$ be the Euclidean volume form on $\mathbb{R}^n$. The outward unit normal to $S^{n-1}$ at $p$ is $N(p)=p$ (the position vector, since the sphere has radius $1$). The induced Riemannian volume form on $S^{n-1}$ is
$$\sigma = \iota_N \omega,$$
i.e. $\sigma_p(v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}) = \omega_p\bigl(N(p),v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}\bigr) = \omega_p(p,v_1,\dots,v_{n-1})$ for tangent vectors $v_i\in T_p S^{n-1}$.

**Computation of the pullback.** For $p\in S^{n-1}$ and $v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}\in T_p S^{n-1}$,
$$(f^*\sigma)_p(v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}) = \sigma_{Ap}(Av_1,\dots,Av_{n-1}) = \omega_{Ap}\bigl(Ap,\,Av_1,\,\dots,\,Av_{n-1}\bigr).$$
Since $A$ is linear, this is the evaluation of $\omega$ on the $n$ vectors $(Ap, Av_1,\dots,Av_{n-1}) = A(p, v_1,\dots,v_{n-1})$. By the defining property of the determinant form,
$$\omega_{Ap}(Ap,Av_1,\dots,Av_{n-1}) = (\det A)\,\omega_p(p,v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}).$$
Because $A$ is a rotation, $\det A = 1$, so
$$(f^*\sigma)_p(v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}) = \omega_p(p,v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}) = \sigma_p(v_1,\dots,v_{n-1}).$$

**Conclusion.** $f^*\sigma = \sigma$, so the rotation $A$ preserves the volume form on $S^{n-1}$.

The essential ingredients are: (1) orthogonality keeps $S^{n-1}$ invariant and carries the outward normal $N(p)=p$ to $N(Ap)=Ap$; (2) the volume form on $S^{n-1}$ is $\iota_N\omega$; (3) $\det A=1$ makes $A$ preserve $\omega$. The same argument shows more generally that any $A\in O(n)$ with $\det A=+1$ preserves $\sigma$, while an orientation-reversing orthogonal map ($\det A=-1$) would send $\sigma\mapsto -\sigma$.

